# Installer, uninstaller and systemd unit

- Windows is for development only. The daemon warns at startup that data-directory ACLs are unchecked on Windows.
- `scripts/install.sh` runs as root on other people's Ubuntu/Debian hosts (amd64, arm64, systemd), usually piped from curl: no prompts, everything in functions with one grouped call on the last line (a truncated download must be a syntax error), commands that could read stdin get `</dev/null`. Its `readonly RELEASE_VERSION=''` line is stamped by `scripts/release.sh`; keep it exactly that.
- Order: validate options (TLS pair matches with `openssl` when present), then every host check (root, OS, arch, systemd, ports, Docker state and the release code name when the repository is to be added, download, SHA-256, archive contents, binary runs and reports the expected version) before the first change. A failed download or checksum must leave the host untouched. What can still fail after changes began (packages, Docker's key or candidates, the service start) is listed in `docs/INSTALL.md`; keep that list true. Payloads come from a release download (`SHA256SUMS` or `--sha256`), `--archive` (refused without `--sha256` or a `SHA256SUMS` entry next to it; copied into the work directory first, and only that copy is hashed, listed and extracted) or `--binary` (the unverified path for source checkouts; unit from `deploy/`, uninstaller from `scripts/`); all are installed from a private `mktemp -d`. Archives must hold exactly `LICENSE golem-deploy golem-deploy.service uninstall.sh` as plain files; modes are always set by the installer, never taken from the archive. `GOLEM_DEPLOY_RELEASE_URL` (https or file) replaces the download directory. curl: `--proto =https --proto-redir =https --tlsv1.2`, retries in `fetch`, and size caps enforced while writing (`head -c`), because older curl ignores `--max-filesize` without a length.
- Docker: a working Docker is kept. Missing: Docker's apt repository per Docker's install pages (`/etc/apt/keyrings/docker.asc`, deb822 `/etc/apt/sources.list.d/docker.sources` with `Signed-By`), written only after the key's fingerprint matched (always checked; the packages step installs `gpg` when missing). An existing repository is reused; `docker_repo_configured` counts only enabled entries in `sources.list`, `*.list` and `*.sources` (no backups, comments or `Enabled: no`). After `apt-get update`, `apt-cache policy` must show `download.docker.com` for every candidate (`docker_candidates_ok`). Packages `docker-ce docker-ce-cli containerd.io docker-buildx-plugin`; every `apt-get install` uses `--no-remove`. Stopped `docker-ce`: start it. Conflicting distribution packages with no working Docker: stop with the `apt-get remove` command, never remove packages. Missing buildx: install `docker-buildx-plugin` only with `docker-ce` and Docker's repository. `--no-docker` disables all installs. Snap and rootless Docker stay unsupported. Keep the conflict list and steps in sync with Docker's pages.
- Idempotent: a re-run replaces binary, unit, uninstaller and TLS copies, rewrites `/etc/sysctl.d/60-golem-deploy.conf` (never lowering a higher value; verify by reading `/proc/sys`, since `sysctl -p` exits 0 on refusal), resets the unit's start limit and restarts; it never rewrites an existing env file or touches an existing data directory (it refuses one with the wrong owner or mode). When the service does not start and this run wrote the env file, it stops the service and moves the file to `golem-deploy.env.failed`.
- A fresh install with neither listen override nor TLS files uses 443 plus an HTTP listener on 80 when both are free, otherwise a loopback port. Without `--public-host`, a public outgoing address from `ip route get` becomes `GOLEM_DEPLOY_PUBLIC_HOST`; private addresses leave it empty with a hint. The env file holds only `GOLEM_DEPLOY_*` variables of `serve` (`cmd/golem-deploy/main.go`). When a serve flag, its default, the data directory rules (`internal/datadir`), the plaintext/loopback rule or the port-range rules change, update the script's validation, its printed next steps and `docs/INSTALL.md`.
- Next steps come from the effective env file, including the data directory (`GOLEM_DEPLOY_DATA_DIR` when absolute) for `admin setup-link` and the printed commands. The first admin step relies on `golem-deploy admin setup-link [--data-dir] [--origin] [--ttl]` (one line `<origin>/setup#t=<token>` on stdout; exit 0, 3 admin exists, 1 error, 2 usage or an older binary), run as the service user after the service started. The link is a secret: keep it in a variable, never in a file or the journal; accept only a 43-character `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` token; turn `xtrace` off from the link's creation to the end of `main` and restore it. Pipelines under `pipefail` must not let a reader exit early (no `head` or `grep -q` after a writer that could get SIGPIPE). Fallback: the `admin create` command (must match its flags, run as the service user). The managed-TLS fingerprint is read from this start's journal line `panel self-signed TLS fingerprint` (`internal/certs/panel.go`); keep the parser in sync. It never changes firewall rules; with ufw active it prints `ufw allow` lines.
- `scripts/uninstall.sh` (installed as `/usr/local/sbin/golem-deploy-uninstall`) surveys first (refusing mounts at or below a directory it deletes, from `/proc/self/mountinfo`), then stops the service and checks that it stopped, then (purge) lists and deletes Docker objects, checking every listing's exit status, and only when all of that succeeded removes unit, binary, cache, sysctl file, data and settings directories, user and itself. A failed listing or deletion keeps everything else and prints the retry command with `--instance`. Docker objects are deleted only with a known instance id: `--instance`, then the installed binary's `admin instance-id` (run as the service user; exit 1 not initialised, 2 usage or an older binary), then `sqlite3 -readonly`; unknown with managed objects present stops and lists the ids with counts. Containers: managed and instance labels plus any of the daemon-only keys `server-id`, `inspector-hub`, `helper` (union of queries; build images never carry them). Images: only `golem-deploy/` tags with `--no-prune`, the id only when untagged, never `-f`; images with other tags or used by other containers are left and reported. Files and links are removed with `rm -f` (a directory found there is left with a warning); directories with `rm -rf --one-file-system`. It prints what it will delete and needs a typed `purge` on a terminal or `--yes`. Never prune, never remove Docker, its repository or packages, never delete a data directory other than `/var/lib/golem-deploy`. It must cope with half-removed installations. Keep its labels and the image namespace in sync with `internal/docker` (`validate.go`, `data.go`, `build.go`).
- The unit's hardening must stay compatible with what the daemon does: run `git`, `git-lfs`, `ssh`, the docker CLI (including `docker build` through the buildx plugin, whose build client config and state are under `<data-dir>/docker-build` and scratch files in the private `/tmp`; the build runs in dockerd) and itself (askpass); connect to `/var/run/docker.sock`; bind TCP/UDP to probe game ports; write only below the data directory (plus the non-build `DOCKER_CONFIG` in the cache directory); pass env files to docker as an inherited pipe (`/dev/fd/3`). Every directive has a comment saying why; drop a directive rather than leave it unexplained. Paths handed to dockerd (bind sources) must be the real host paths, so no `TemporaryFileSystem=`/`BindPaths=` over the data directory and no `/tmp` paths. Game containers live in dockerd's cgroup: stopping the unit must never stop them, and `TimeoutStopSec` must cover the daemon's 20-second drain and store close (`internal/httpapi/server.go`, `internal/daemon/daemon.go`). The unit grants only `CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE`.
- Verification: `shellcheck scripts/*.sh` (CI job "Shell"); `systemd-analyze verify` on a changed unit; end to end in systemd-booted, privileged `ubuntu:24.04` and `debian:12` containers with volumes for the nested `/var/lib/docker` and `/var/lib/containerd` (the installer installs Docker inside), installing from `scripts/release.sh` output through `GOLEM_DEPLOY_RELEASE_URL=file://...`: checksum mismatch, truncated or tampered archives, Docker missing, `--no-docker`, re-runs, an existing env file, loopback and network listeners, every setup-link exit code (a small stand-in executable can force them), `--binary`, `--archive` (no checksum, a file swapped after the copy), a download without a length over the cap, Docker's key with `gpg` missing and a wrong key (a local `openssl s_server -WWW` behind an `/etc/hosts` entry and a trusted test CA), an apt pin that wins over Docker's repository, backup or disabled Docker sources, a failed first start, `bash -x`, uninstall, `--purge` with and without a terminal (`script -qec` gives one), with the id from `admin instance-id` (a stand-in), `--instance`, `sqlite3` and unknown, another instance's objects, an old container without `inspector-hub`, a failing Docker deletion and its retry, a mount below the data directory, operator-tagged and derived images, and a half-removed installation. Name test objects with a unique prefix, remove exactly those afterwards and never prune.
